    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Research-Assistant-Professor---Career_R-15396": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Professor - Career",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-20\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: November 19, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 155-NN_FACULTY - Research Assistant Professor - Career, 155-YN_FACULTY - Research Assistant Professor - Career Business Title: Research Assistant Professor - Career Employee Type: Faculty (+) (Fixed Term) Position Term: 12 Month Position Details: The Opportunity: The Department of Entomology at Washington State University invites applications for a full-time Research Assistant Professor, career-track faculty position in Pullman, Washington, specializing in economic entomology and ecological pest management. The successful candidate will use digital agriculture, predictive modeling, and research on resilience to weather variability to develop practical tools that help Washington growers rapidly and precisely manage insect pests, pathogens, and diseases across specialty-crop systems. This is a 12-month, fixed-term appointment for 1 year with the possibility of renewal, contingent upon satisfactory performance and continued availability of grant and contract funding. The anticipated start date for this appointment is during the Spring 2027 semester, subject to departmental needs and hiring timelines. Job duties: The anticipated workload is 100% Research. The successful candidate will design and conduct research addressing pest population dynamics, phenology, integrated pest management, and the effects of weather and climate variability on agricultural systems. The position will integrate field-monitoring, environmental, and crop data with digital agriculture, artificial intelligence, and predictive modeling to develop and validate practical decision support tools for growers. The candidate will conduct research across multiple cropping systems, including potato, tree fruit, small fruits, grains, hops, alfalfa, and vegetable seed crops; publish findings in peer-reviewed outlets; present results to scientific and stakeholder audiences; collaborate with growers, commodity groups, Extension professionals, agencies, and interdisciplinary research teams; contribute to grant and contract proposals and project reporting; and mentor students or research personnel as appropriate. Additional Information: Annual Salary: $70,000 - $86,000 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: An earned Ph.D. in a life-science discipline by the position start date. Demonstrated skills and expertise in phenology modeling, integrated pest management, insect biology, pesticide chemistry and application, digital agriculture, artificial intelligence, and climate-change research. Demonstrated expertise working across multiple agricultural cropping systems, including but not limited to potato, tree fruit, small fruits, grains, hops, alfalfa, and vegetable seed crops. Demonstrated ability to conduct independent research and work collaboratively with interdisciplinary teams and agricultural stakeholders. Demonstrated written and oral communication skills. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated research experience in economic entomology, ecological pest management, or a closely related field. Experience integrating field-monitoring, environmental, weather, climate, and crop data to develop or validate predictive pest-management models. Experience developing practical digital decision-support tools or recommendations for agricultural producers. Experience with statistical analysis, geographic information systems, remote sensing, sensor data, or programming tools such as R or Python. Knowledge of specialty-crop production systems important to Washington State or the Pacific Northwest. Record of peer-reviewed publications appropriate to the candidate’s career level. Experience preparing competitive grant or contract proposals and supporting externally funded research projects. Experience collaborating with growers, commodity organizations, government agencies, Extension professionals, and interdisciplinary research teams. Experience communicating research findings to technical and nontechnical audiences through publications, presentations, workshops, reports, or digital platforms. Experience mentoring or supervising students, postdoctoral researchers, or other research personnel. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: May be eligible for work visa sponsorship About WSU, Pullman, CAHNRS, and the Department of Entomology: Washington State University is a land-grant, multiple-campus Research 1 institution. This position will serve on the Pullman campus, which has an enrollment of approximately 20,000 students and is located in southeastern Washington on the homelands of the Nimíipuu (Nez Perce) Tribe and Palus people. Located about 80 miles south of Spokane, Pullman is also near the Idaho panhandle and Moscow Mountain. The rolling hills of the Palouse offer a wide range of activities and a four season climate. The area provides access to the cultural and academic resources of both Washington State University and the University of Idaho in neighboring Moscow, Idaho. For information about the region, visit https://pullmanchamber.com. The College of Agricultural, Human, and Natural Resource Sciences provides research, teaching, and Extension programs that support Washington agriculture, communities, natural resources, and human well-being. Learn more at https://cahnrs.wsu.edu. For more than 50 years, the WSU Department of Entomology has contributed solutions to important agricultural and ecological problems in Washington and beyond. Its faculty lead nationally recognized programs in biological control, arthropod integrated pest management and education, pollinator biology, decision-support systems, and pesticide education. Faculty are located in Pullman and at Research and Extension Centers across Washington, providing access to laboratories, grower fields, and diverse specialty-crop systems. Learn more at https://entomology.wsu.edu. Area/College: College of Agricultural, Human, and Natural Resource Sciences (CAHNRS) Department Name: Department of Entomology Location: Pullman, WA Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1) A cover letter addressing qualifications for this position as well as a response to the following prompt: As a land-grant university, our university has a continuing commitment to ensuring equitable and meaningful inclusion in our classrooms, collaborations, and academic work. The successful candidate for this faculty position will have the experiences, knowledge, and skills to build their research in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Highlight how your qualifications include: the development of digital agriculture tools and predictive models of IPM; experience conducting applied research on pest management under changing environmental conditions; provided research-based support across diverse cropping systems; and secured and managed grant-funding to support research. 2) Curriculum vitae 3) In addition to the cover letter, please provide a Research statement that outlines your scholarly work and includes your future plans. Inquiries should be directed to Dave Crowder: dcrowder@wsu.edu External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Associate-or-Assistant-Professor---Tenure-Track---Isotope-Geochemistry--Pullman--WA-_R-15562": {
      "title": "Associate or Assistant Professor - Tenure Track | Isotope Geochemistry (Pullman, WA)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-13\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: December 13, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 270-NN_FACULTY - Assistant Professor, 280-NN_FACULTY - Associate Professor, 396-NN_FACULTY - New Teaching Track - Pre-Academic Year Business Title: Associate or Assistant Professor - Tenure Track | Isotope Geochemistry (Pullman, WA) Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 9 Month - Summer Position Details: The Opportunity: The School of the Environment at Washington State University College of Arts & Sciences invites applications for a permanent, full-time, nine-month, tenure-track Assistant or Tenure-eligible Associate Professor position in Pullman, Washington. The successful Associate Professor candidate will have an expedited tenure review upon acceptance of the position. We seek a scholar with expertise in radiogenic isotopes geochemsitry and lithospheric dynamics whose research advances understanding of Earth processes and has applications to economic geology, critical mineral resources, energy transition technologies, or other energy futures research. A strong applicant will make extensive use of our state-of-the-art Geochemical Laboratory while guiding it into its next stage of growth and building upon the school's existing strengths in Earth science research and education. The established and highly regarded WSU Geochemical Laboratories includes our Radiogenic Isotope and Geochronology Laboratory (RIGL) and the Peter Hooper GeoAnalytical Laboratory (GAL). The successful candidate will contribute to a collaborative and interdisciplinary research environment that advances applications of isotopes geochemistry and supports research relevant to critical minerals, energy resources, and sustainable energy futures. The successful candidate will play a leadership role in the Geochemical Laboratory, support its broad user community, and advance their scientific impact through innovative research, strategic partnerships, and external funding. It is anticipated that the successful candidate will begin the appointment on August 16, 2027 . Job duties: A successful candidate will be expected to develop and maintain an internationally recognized and externally funded research program focusing on isotopic geochemistry, lithospheric processes, and other Earth systems. They will support the strategic growth of the Geochemical Laboratory, advancing capabilities through the development of new applications and instrumentation. The candidate will be expected to teach both undergraduate and graduate courses and supervise undergraduate and graduate students in research projects. Finally, they will be expected to contribute to the School of the Environment's growth through curriculum development, graduate student mentorship, recruitment, and assessment activities. Lab Overview: The Geochemical Laboratory within the School of the Environment incorporates a range of geochemical equipment run by our staff of excellent technicians. This lab includes a Thermo Scientific Neptune Plus MC-ICPMS, a Thermo Fisher Scientific Element 2 ICP-MS with an Analyte Excite 193-nm ArF Excimer laser, an Agilent 8900 ICP-MS/MS with an ESI 193 nm laser system, an Agilent 7700 ICP-MS, a Class 1000 HEPA-filtered clean lab for sample preparation, a JEOL JXA8500F Field Emission electron probe, and a ThermoARL Perform'X WDXRF. These facilities are consistently used for whole rock geochemical analysis geochronology, thermochronology, elemental microanalysis, and major and trace element mapping. Additional Information: 9-Month Academic Year Salary : Assistant Professor: $85,000.00 - $95,000.00 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications Associate Professor : $96,000.00 - $110,000.00 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position or as mandated by a U.S. Department of Labor prevailing wage determination. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications for All Ranks : Completed PhD in Geology, Earth Science or adjacent disciplines. Demonstrated record of laboratory management. Demonstrated potential for external funding. Demonstrated potential for successful teaching and instruction. Demonstration of potential to support access and opportunity in research, teaching, and service in alignment with WSU’s land-grant mission. Additional Required Qualifications for Associate Professor Rank : Demonstrated record of external funding. Demonstrated record of successful supervision of graduate students. Proven successful teaching and instruction of upper-level undergraduate courses. Demonstrated record of supporting access and opportunity in research, teaching, and service in alignment with WSU’s land-grant mission. Currently serving in the rank of an Associate Professor or equivalent and has a record of accomplishments in research, teaching commensurate with department and college standards for tenure at the level of associate professor. CAS T&P Guidelines: https://cas.wsu.edu/faculty/documents/2025/07/cas-promotion-and-tenure-guidelines.pdf/ Preferred Qualifications for All Ranks : Demonstrated experience in the supervision of graduate students. Experience implementing clean laboratory SOPs. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: May be eligible for work visa sponsorship. Contact for Inquiries : Sarah Roley ( sarah.roley@wsu.edu ) Area/College: College of Arts and Sciences Department Name: School of the Environment Location: Pullman, WA About WSU, Pullman, CAS, and the School of the Environment: Washington State University is a land-grant, multiple-campus Research 1 institution. This position will serve on the Pullman campus, which has an enrollment of around 20,000 students and is located in Southeastern Washington on the homelands of the Nimíipuu (Nez Perce) Tribe and Palus people. Located 80 miles south of metropolitan Spokane, Pullman is also a quick drive away from the scenic Idaho panhandle and Moscow Mountain. The rolling hills of the Palouse offer a wide range of activities and a true four-season climate. The area provides ample opportunity to enjoy the cultural and academic hub of both Washington State University and the University of Idaho, in the neighboring town of Moscow, Idaho. For more information about the region, please refer to https://pullmanchamber.com . Encompassing more than 19 departments, schools, programs, and 19 centers, institutes, museums, and research stations across five campuses statewide, the College of Arts & Sciences (CAS) is the heart of WSU. Together over 700 CAS faculty and staff deliver more than 50% of WSU's total undergraduate and graduate instruction, including the vast majority of WSU's core curriculum. We drive disciplinary and transdisciplinary research, scholarship, and creative activities across the arts, humanities, social sciences, sciences, and mathematics. The College of Arts & Sciences seeks to lead a reimagining of WSU's land-grant mission for the 21st century, expanding the boundaries of creativity and discovery while simultaneously recognizing more completely our oblications to the State of Washington and the communities we serve. For more about the College of Arts & Sciences at WSU, please refer to https://cas.wsu.edu . The School of the Environment (SOE) is a multidisciplinary unit working to advance earth, environmental, and geosciences to better understand global ecology, ecosystem science, and sustainability of both the natural and human-built world. SOE incorporates four primary pillars including Earth Science, Environmental and Ecosystem Sciences, Wildlife Ecology & Conservation Sciences, and Forest Ecology and Management. Faculty, students, and staff engage in innovative research spanning Earth history, conservation science, climate change, hydrology, geoscience, environmental policy, and natural resource management. The School of the Environment offers undergraduate and graduate degree programs that allow students to address complex challenges through scientific inquiry, collaboration, and hands-on learning. Students have opportunities to participate in field-based education, laboratory research, internships, and community-engaged projects while working alongside faculty and our external partners. Through its programs, research centers, and partnerships across Washington State and beyond, the SOE is committed to developing the next generation of scientists, helping to advance solutions that support resource management, sustainability, resilient ecosystems, and informed environmental stewardship. For more about the the SOE at WSU, please refer to https://environment.wsu.edu .   WSU acknowledges that its locations statewide are on the homelands of Native American peoples, who have lived in this region and have been caretakers of the land from time immemorial. The Morrill Act of 1862 established our land-grant institution by providing public and federal lands that are traced back to the dispossession of Indigenous lands, often taken by coercive and violent acts, and the disregard of treaties. For that, we extend our deepest apologies. We owe our deepest gratitude to the Native peoples of this region and maintain our commitment towards reconciliation. And as a land-grant institution, WSU is deeply committed to the land-grant mission, its Memorandums of Understanding with Native tribes in the region, and a tradition of service to society.To read the full WSU land acknowledgement, please refer to https://wsu.edu/about/wsu-land-acknowledgement/ . Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1) Cover Letter addressing qualifications for this position as well as a response to the following prompt: As a land-grant university, we have a commitment to ensure access and opportunity in all aspects of our work. The successful candidate for this faculty position will have the experiences, knowledge, and skills to build their research/scholarship/creative practice, pedagogy, curriculum, and service in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Using the job announcement for this position, please list and explain up to three areas of responsibility you see as related to fulfilling this mission. 2) Curriculum vitae 3) Teaching statement that describes your teaching experience and philosophy, which may incorporate experience from formal or informal teaching and mentoring opportunities 4) Research statement that outlines your scholarly work and includes your future plans External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as PDF, if possible. Reference contact information will be requested later in the recruitment process through Workday. Note: Three (3) references are required at Assistant Professor rank and four (4) references are required at Associate Professor rank. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_R-15535": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-09\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: October 25, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 306-YY_FACULTY - Postdoctoral Research Associate Business Title: Postdoctoral Research Associate Employee Type: Faculty (+) (Fixed Term) Position Term: 12 Month Position Details: The Opportunity: The School of Food Science at Washington State University (WSU) invites applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate specializing in Extrusion Processing, Protein Chemistry, and Ingredient Functionality. The successful candidate will join the Ganjyal Food Processing Laboratory, a nationally recognized extrusion and food processing research program, to lead applied and fundamental research on twin-screw and single-screw extrusion of cereal, pulse, and plant protein-based food systems. The position offers the opportunity to work at pilot scale, connect processing engineering with protein and ingredient chemistry, collaborate closely with food industry partners and commodity groups, and translate research findings into practical solutions for food processors through WSU’s land-grant extension mission. Key Responsibilities: Design and lead interdisciplinary research on twin-screw and single-screw extrusion processing, linking process engineering parameters to protein and starch transformations, rheological behavior, and final product structure and quality. Operate, maintain, and troubleshoot laboratory-scale extrusion and associated processing equipment, and train students in safe and reproducible operation. Characterize ingredients, melts, and extrudates using rheological, protein chemistry, thermal, and microstructural methods. Prepare manuscripts for peer-reviewed journals, write sponsor reports, contribute to grant proposals, and present research at scientific and industry meetings. Mentor graduate and undergraduate students in laboratory and pilot plant research, experimental design, and data analysis. Support industry collaborations and extension programming for food processors. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . For information or questions about Benefits, please contact HRS Benefits at 509-335-4521. Additional Information: Annual Salary: $62,232 to $70,000 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. This is a full-time ( 100% FTE), temporary position. This position is overtime eligible. Appointment Duration: This is a temporary position expected to start on January 1, 2027, and end on December 31, 2027. Renewal is dependent on the need for extension and/or if additional funding is secured to extend employment. Postdoctoral Requirements: For detailed information on eligibility for postdoctoral appointments, please visit: WSU/UAW Postdoctoral Collective Bargaining Agreement. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Food Science, Food Engineering, Biological Systems Engineering, Cereal Science, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated hands-on operating experience with both twin-screw and single-screw extruders, with in-depth understanding of the engineering and chemistry principles governing extrusion processing. Demonstrated expertise in food rheology and its application to understanding raw material behavior, melt flow, and product structure. Demonstrated expertise in protein chemistry, including protein structure, functionality, and changes during processing. Thorough understanding of food ingredient chemistry and functionality, including proteins, starches, and fibers. Evidence of successful peer-reviewed publications as first author and strong scientific writing and oral presentation skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively within an interdisciplinary team. Preferred Qualifications: Experience with protein texturization, including high moisture meat analogs or texturized vegetable proteins, and with pulse, cereal, or pseudocereal ingredients. Experience with complementary analytical techniques such as electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE), Fourier Transform Infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC), pasting analysis (RVA), texture analysis, and microstructural imaging (SEM or X-ray scattering). Experience with design of experiments and statistical or multivariate analysis using tools such as R, Python, Statistical Analysis System (SAS), or JMP. Experience contributing to grant proposals and project reports. Experience mentoring graduate or undergraduate students. Experience working with industry partners or delivering technical outreach to food processors.   Position Sponsorship Eligibility: Not eligible for work visa sponsorship About Department/College/Area/ Campus - School of Food Science Area/College: College of Agricultural, Human, and Natural Resource Sciences Department Name: School of Food Science Location: Pullman, Washington Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1) Resume or Curriculum Vitae, including a list of Publications 2) Cover Letter External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. All candidates , documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Reference contact information will be requested later in the recruitment process through Workday. Contact: For questions regarding this position or the application process, please contact Girish Ganjyal, girish.ganjyal@wsu.edu. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Union Representation: This position is represented exclusively by Postdocs United/United Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers of America (UAW) under the Collective Bargaining Agreement (CBA). Information regarding the Union and the CBA is available on the  WSU Labor Relations website. Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Clinical-Pathology---Clinical-Career-Track---Open-Rank_R-15325": {
      "title": "Clinical Pathology | Clinical Career Track | Open Rank",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-07\nPosted: Posted 14 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: November 8, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 164-NN_FACULTY - Clinical Assistant Professor - Career Business Title: Clinical Pathology | Clinical Career Track | Open Rank Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 12 Month Position Details: The Opportunity: The Washington State University (WSU) Department of Veterinary Clinical Sciences (VCS) in the College of Veterinary Medicine (CVM) seeks to fill a full-time continuous career track (clinical sub-track) A ssistant or Associate Professor faculty position within the Clinical Pathology section of the Veterinary Teaching Hospital (VTH). The position is available immediately. The successful applicant will be expected to provide diagnostic service for the VTH; clinical and didactic instruction of professional veterinary students, interns, residents, and graduate students. The opportunity to conduct research/scholarship within the candidate’s area of interest is available. The distribution of effort between clinical service, teaching, scholarly effort and academic service/outreach is negotiable. The successful applicant must be able to effectively communicate and work with people of diverse backgrounds The successful applicant will join one to two other full-time board-certified clinical pathologists and a dedicated team of two residents, one laboratory manager, two certified medical laboratory scientists, two laboratory assistants, and multiple, trained, DVM student employees. The well-equipped Clinical Pathology Laboratory ( https://hospital.vetmed.wsu.edu/hospital-wide-services/clinical-pathology/ ) processes over 15,000 patient samples per year. Additional Information: Annual Salary: Assistant Professor: $145,000 - $152,000 Associate Professor: $160,000 - $170,000 In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Qualifications for all ranks: Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM, VMD) degree or a related foreign equivalent degree (such as a BVSc) Board-certified or board-eligible in Clinical Pathology by the American College of Veterinary Pathology or the European College of Veterinary Pathology by the time of hire. Considerations regarding faculty rank will be determined based on the interests and qualifications of the candidate meeting CVM’s promotion guidelines, as will division of effort. Candidates for Associate Professor should have evidence of progressively higher levels of experience and expertise in the provision of outstanding clinical service, clinical teaching, and/or research/scholarship. For more information, see the CVM’s promotion guidelines: ( https://provost.wsu.edu/documents/2023/03/vet-med-promotion-and-tenure-guidelines.pdf ).  Position Sponsorship Eligibility: Eligible for work visa sponsorship Application Information: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1) Cover letter summarizing the candidate’s qualifications, stating the desired faculty rank 2) Summary of the candidate’s clinical, teaching, scholarly, and outreach and community engagement interests and goals 3) Curriculum vitae Questions regarding the position may be directed to Dr. Jillian Haines, Chair of the Search Committee, by email ( jmhaines@wsu.edu ) or by telephone (509) 335-0711.   Area Overview: The WSU CVM is organized into five departments/schools, including VCS, Veterinary Microbiology and Pathology (VMP), Integrative Physiology and Neuroscience (IPN), the School of Molecular Biosciences (SMB), and the Paul G. Allen School for Global Health, each dedicated to excellence in teaching, research, and service ( http://www.vetmed.wsu.edu ). We are also home to a tier-one Veterinary National Laboratory (Washington Animal Disease Diagnostic Laboratory ( https://waddl.vetmed.wsu.edu/ ) with multiple board-certified anatomic pathologists and microbiologists. The VTH is a full-service referral and primary care center that aspires to be the country’s best academic veterinary hospital for patients, students, clients, colleagues, and community. The joint mission of the VTH and VCS is to improve the health and well-being of all animals through excellence in medical care, training future veterinarians, and finding innovative ways to diagnose and treat disease. Dedicated to this vision and mission are more than 60 full-time faculty members and 170 staff and house officers. The VTH is a leading referral center in the state of Washington and the Pacific Northwest with over 20,000 patient visits per year as a regional tertiary care facility which provides the Clinical Pathology service with a broad-based caseload. The Clinical Pathology Laboratory provides clinical diagnostic services and professional laboratory expertise nationally and internationally to veterinary hospitals, referring veterinarians, and referring laboratories. Our services include state of the art automated hematology, coagulation, clinical chemistry, blood-gas, and immunoassay analyzers as well as fluid analysis, cytology, and urinalysis which are performed by certified medical laboratory scientists and clinical pathology trained veterinary medicine students. Diagnostic services are provided for multiple species of animals including equine, bovine, canine, feline, exotics, camelids, caprine and others. In addition to services provided on site, services are provided nationally via overnight mail which can be utilized by our clients and customers for comprehensive testing. The clinical pathology laboratory is dedicated to educating current and future veterinarians through pre-clinical teaching, clinical rotations, and residency and graduate student training ( https://vetmed.wsu.edu/education/residencies-internships/combined-clinical-pathology-residency/ ) as well as providing services for faculty and resident research projects. WSU Overview: Washington State University is a land-grant institution with an enrollment of approximately 20,000 students on the Pullman campus and 23,000 students system-wide.  The University of Idaho, also a land-grant university, is located in Moscow, Idaho, 8 miles from the Pullman campus.  These communities offer a friendly, small-town living environment and access to a rich assortment of cultural, recreational, and athletic attractions.  Pullman has a four-season climate, clean air, good schools, and a low crime rate.  Pullman (population 34,000) is about 75 miles south of Spokane, Washington, and lies close to recreational areas in Washington, Oregon, Idaho, Montana, and British Columbia. To learn more about the Pullman community, visit:  http://www.pullmanchamber.com . The university’s web page, www.wsu.edu , offers additional information about WSU and the surrounding area. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Teaching-Assistant-Professor---Career_R-15329": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant Professor – Career",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-06\nPosted: Posted 15 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: November 15, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 158-NN_FACULTY - Teaching Assistant Professor - Career Business Title: Teaching Assistant Professor – Career Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 9 Month Position Details: The Opportunity: The School of Economics Sciences is dedicated to high quality teaching, research, outreach, and engagement. Our program provides students with the economic knowledge, quantitative tools, and communications skills necessary to secure professional positions in a wide range of industries and public organizations.  As a Teaching Assistant Professor with the School of Economic Sciences with the WSU College of Agricultural, Human, and Natural Resource Sciences (CAHNRS) your primary responsibilities will be teaching economics courses to undergraduate students, with minimal research expectations that may focus on teaching pedagogy or any field within economics. The successful candidate will be a dynamic and engaging instructor, capable of inspiring and attracting students to the discipline of economics. Additional duties include advising students, collaborating with faculty and staff across related disciplines, and actively participating in committees, instructional activities, and other initiatives within the School of Economic Sciences, the Pullman campus, and Washington State University. You will also have opportunities to mentor undergraduate students and supervise their research projects. Your teaching load will be three courses per semester (3/3), with 85% teaching, 5% research, and an additional 10% devoted to services. Additional Information: This is a full-time (100% FTE), 9-month appointment position . This position works on-site the WSU Pullman campus in Pullman, WA. Annual Salary: $60,000 - $90,000 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: Earned Ph.D. in Economics or a related discipline at date of hire Demonstrated record of, or potential for, ensuring access and opportunity in all aspects of our educational experiences in alignment with WSU’s land-grant mission. Preferred Qualifications: Evidence of strong training in theory and applicable quantitative methods Evidence of excellent communication skills, both written and verbal Demonstrated ability to teach effectively at the university level Position Sponsorship Eligibility: May be eligible for work visa sponsorship About the School of Economic Sciences - https://ses.wsu.edu/   The School of Economic Sciences offers a highly rated PhD program in Economics and Agricultural Economics, an M.S. degree in Economics, a professionally oriented Master of Applied Economics, and an undergraduate program leading to B.S. and B.A. degrees (both in-person in Pullman and fully online) that can open doors to a wide variety of career paths. The School is dedicated to high-quality teaching, research, outreach, and engagement. Students are taught by 30 full-time faculty members from many economic disciplines. The faculty and students are actively engaged in cutting-edge research. There is a strong tradition of excellence in applied econometric modeling. About the College of Agricultural, Human, and Natural Resource Sciences (CAHNRS) - https://cahnrs.wsu.edu/   Department Name: School of Economic Sciences- SES Location: Pullman, Washington Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Letter of Application 3) Teaching Statements 4) Research Statement with at least one example of your research appended to the research statement External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Reference contact information will be requested later in the recruitment process through Workday. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Assistant-Associate-Full-Professor-in-Veterinary-Diagnostic-Imaging_R-14661": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Veterinary Diagnostic Imaging",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: November 1, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 164-NN_FACULTY - Clinical Assistant Professor - Career Business Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Veterinary Diagnostic Imaging Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 12 Month Position Details: The Opportunity: The Washington State University (WSU) Department of Veterinary Clinical Sciences (VCS) in the College of Veterinary Medicine (CVM) seeks to fill an open rank faculty position in Veterinary Diagnostic Imaging. This position can be continuous career track or permanent tenure track, depending on the preference and qualifications of the applicant. Candidates will have the opportunity to adjust their schedule to satisfy personal or professional needs outside their appointment, including external consultation services if desired. The successful applicant will provide diagnostic service for the VTH as well as clinical and didactic instruction of professional veterinary students and house officers. Opportunities for collaborative or independent research/scholarship will be available if interested. Expectations for scholarship/research will be greater for a successful tenure track applicant, appropriate to rank. The successful applicant will work in a collaborative environment alongside our many specialty clinicians as cases are interpreted and managed. The successful applicant will join one other board-certified faculty radiologist as well as a dedicated team of licensed veterinary technicians and technician assistants. Small animal ultrasound duties are shared between the Diagnostic Imaging and Small Animal Internal Medicine Services. The Diagnostic Imaging Service is further supported by a highly successful partnership with a private teleradiology service that provides clinical teaching of fourth-year DVM students and diagnostic interpretations for the VTH.  Diagnostic imaging provides support to nearly every small animal and large animal service within the WSU Veterinary Teaching Hospital (VTH) in Pullman, Washington. Our VTH is the leading referral center in the state of Washington and the Pacific Northwest with over 20,000 patient visits per year, aspiring to be the country’s best academic veterinary hospital for patients, students, clients, colleagues, and community. The joint mission of the VTH and VCS is to improve the health and well-being of all animals through excellence in medical care, training future veterinarians, and finding innovative ways to diagnose and treat disease. Dedicated to this vision and mission are more than 60 full-time faculty members and 170 staff and house officers. The WSU CVM is organized into five departments/schools, including VCS, Veterinary Microbiology and Pathology (VMP), Integrative Physiology and Neuroscience (IPN), the School of Molecular Biosciences (SMB), and the Paul G. Allen School for Global Health, each dedicated to excellence in teaching, research, and service ( http://www.vetmed.wsu.edu ). We are also home to a tier-one Veterinary National Laboratory (Washington Animal Disease Diagnostic Laboratory ( https://waddl.vetmed.wsu.edu/ ). Questions regarding the position may be directed to Dr. Jillian Haines, Chair of the Search Committee, by email jmhaines@wsu.edu or by telephone (509) 335-0768.    Additional Information: This is a full time (100% FTE), continuous career track or permanent tenure track position. This position is overtime exempt. Annual Salary: Assistant Professor: $150,000 - $165,000 Associate Professor: $165,000 - $180,000 Full Professor: $180,000 - $195,000 In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: Must possess a Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM) degree or foreign equivalent degree (i.e., BVSc). Board-certified or board-eligible in Radiology/Imaging by the American College of Veterinary Radiology or the European College of Veterinary Diagnostic Imaging by the time of hire. Able to effectively communicate and work collegially and collaboratively with people from all backgrounds. Demonstrate a commitment and relevant ability to foster community outreach, access and engagement. For tenure track consideration, candidates must demonstrate a significant record of (or potential for) research/scholarship. Applicants for tenure track assistant professor must demonstrate potential to establish and maintain a program of scholarship.  Applicants for tenure track associate and full professor must have an active extramurally-funded research program with a record of peer-reviewed publications and national/international recognition. For career track applicants, considerations regarding rank will be determined based on the interests and qualifications of the candidate. Applicants for associate and full professor must have a higher level of clinical and teaching experience, scholarship productivity, if applicable, and national or international recognition as compared to assistant professor applicants. Considerations regarding rank and faculty track will be determined based on the interests and qualifications of the candidate, as will division of time between teaching, clinical duty, and scholarly activity.  As mentioned, scheduling flexibility to allow for more off-clinic time, including enabling independent consultation, is readily negotiable. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: Not eligible for work visa sponsorship About Department/College/Area/Campus - Veterinary Clinical Sciences | College of Veterinary Medicine | Washington State University Area/College: College of Veterinary Medicine Department Name: Veterinary Clinical Sciences Location: Pullman, WA Application Instructions: Applicants are required to submit an electronic application through www.hrs.wsu.edu/careers including: 1) Cover letter, summarizing the candidate’s qualifications, stating the desired faculty rank and providing a summary of the candidate’s clinical, teaching, scholarly, and outreach and community engagement interests and goals 2) Curriculum vitae 3)  Contact information including email address and phone number for three (3) professional references will be requested in the application External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Assistant-Associate-Professor-Veterinary-Neurology-and-Neurosurgery_R-15092": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor Veterinary Neurology and Neurosurgery",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: November 1, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 270-NN_FACULTY - Assistant Professor, 280-NN_FACULTY - Associate Professor Business Title: Assistant/Associate Professor Veterinary Neurology and Neurosurgery Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 12 Month Position Details: The Opportunity: The Washington State University (WSU) Department of Veterinary Clinical Sciences (VCS) in the College of Veterinary Medicine (CVM) seeks to fill a full-time continuous career track (clinical sub-track) Assistant or Associate Professor faculty position within the Neurology and Neurosurgery section of the Veterinary Teaching Hospital (VTH). The successful applicant will provide clinical service within the Neurology/Neurosurgery service while providing clinical and didactic instruction of professional veterinary students, interns, and residents. Opportunities may exist for scholarship/research within the candidate’s area of interest and consistent with the appointed faculty rank and division of effort. Successful applicants should be able to effectively communicate and work collegially and collaboratively with people of diverse backgrounds. Additional Information: The position is available as soon as a suitable candidate can be identified. Applications received by September 1, 2026, will be considered in the initial review process; however, the search will be open until filled. Posting Closing Date: Applicants must submit their completed application by September 1, 2026 at 12:00 a.m. Annual Salary Range: Assistant Professor: $150,000 - $165,000 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications Associate Professor: $165,000 - $180,000 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation and optional supplemental retirement accounts. For a more detailed summary of benefits offered by WSU for Faculty please review the summary of benefits for WSU Faculty and Total Compensation Required Qualifications: The candidate must possess a Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM, VMD) degree or a related foreign equivalent degree (such as a BVSc), and be board-eligible in the American College of Veterinary Internal Medicine (Neurology) (ACVIM) or the European College of Veterinary Neurology (ECVN), or board-certified, by the date of hire. Considerations regarding faculty rank will be determined based on the interests and qualifications of the candidate, as will division of effort. Candidates for Associate Professor should have evidence of progressively higher levels of experience and expertise in the provision of outstanding clinical service, clinical teaching, and/or research/scholarship. See the College of Veterinary Medicine's Promotion and Tenure Guidelines for more information on faculty rank. Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following r equired documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. Cover letter addressing qualifications for the position, stating the desired faculty rank and providing a summary of the candidate’s clinical, teaching, scholarly, and outreach and community engagement interests and goals; Curriculum vitae Reference contact information   is collected as part of the online application process.   Questions regarding the position may be directed to Dr. Sarah Guess, Chair of the Search Committee, by email sarah.guess@wsu.edu or by telephone (509) 335-0711.   About WSU, Pullman, CVM: Washington State University is a land-grant institution with an enrollment of approximately 20,000 students on the Pullman campus and 23,000 students system-wide.  The University of Idaho, also a land-grant university, is located in Moscow, Idaho, 8 miles from the Pullman campus.  These communities offer a friendly, small-town living environment and access to a rich assortment of cultural, recreational, and athletic attractions.  Pullman has a four-season climate, clean air, good schools, and a low crime rate.  Pullman (population 34,000) is about 75 miles south of Spokane, Washington, and lies close to recreational areas in Washington, Oregon, Idaho, Montana, and British Columbia. To learn more about the Pullman community, visit:  http://www.pullmanchamber.com . The university’s web page, www.wsu.edu , offers additional information about WSU and the surrounding area. The WSU CVM is organized into five departments/schools, including VCS, Veterinary Microbiology and Pathology (VMP), Integrative Physiology and Neuroscience (IPN), the School of Molecular Biosciences (SMB), and the Paul G. Allen School for Global Health, each dedicated to excellence in teaching, research, and service (http://www.vetmed.wsu.edu). We are also home to a tier-one Veterinary National Laboratory (Washington Animal Disease Diagnostic Laboratory ( https://waddl.vetmed.wsu.edu/ ). The VTH is a full-service referral and primary care center that aspires to be the country’s best academic veterinary hospital for patients, students, clients, colleagues, and community. The joint mission of the VTH and VCS is to improve the health and well-being of all animals through excellence in medical care, training future veterinarians, and finding innovative ways to diagnose and treat disease. Dedicated to this vision and mission are more than 60 full-time faculty members and 170 staff and house officers. Successful applicants will join a dynamic team with expertise in primary care practice, internal medicine, emergency and critical care, surgery, cardiology, and oncology. The VTH is a leading referral center in the state of Washington and the Pacific Northwest with over 20,000 patient visits per year as a regional tertiary care facility and provides the ECC service with a broad-based caseload. The Neurology and Neurosurgery service is currently rebuilding and presently comprises a board-certified neurologist, 3 locum neurologists, a dedicated team of one licensed veterinary technician, an assistant, a resident, and a patient care coordinator. Neurodiagnostic capabilities in the VTH include in-house magnetic resonance imaging, computed tomography, and electrodiagnostic equipment. Our surgical facility is equipped with a full range of neurosurgical equipment including an operating microscope, a stereotactic brain biopsy system, and a Nico Myraid for tumor resection. Our physical rehabilitation facility is equipped with an underwater treadmill, electric muscle stimulator, therapeutic laser, therapeutic ultrasound and acupuncture and is under the supervision of an American College of Veterinary Sports Medicine and Rehabilitation (ACVSMR) board-certified specialist. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Assistant-Professor---Tenure-Track---Bioengineering_R-15504": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor | Tenure Track | Bioengineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: November 22, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 270-NN_FACULTY - Assistant Professor Business Title: Assistant Professor | Tenure Track | Bioengineering Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 9 Month - Summer Position Details: The Opportunity: The Gene and Linda Voiland School of Chemical Engineering and Bioengineering (VSCEB) at Washington State University (WSU) invites applications for a full-time, permanent, tenure-track faculty position at the assistant professor level. We anticipate the new hire will assist VSCEB at WSU to further grow our vibrant research and educational programs. The position will be located at the WSU Pullman campus, and the individual hired will be expected to join VSCEB on August 16, 2027. The successful candidate is expected to establish a dynamic, externally funded, nationally recognized research program, teach core and elective undergraduate and graduate courses in bioengineering, mentor a diverse group of students, perform professional service, and publish scholarly work. VSCEB welcomes strong candidates in all areas of bioengineering, particularly candidates who can complement existing departmental strengths in molecular and cellular engineering, synthetic biology, biomanufacturing, tissue engineering, biomechanics, and engineering education and the growing expertise in Artificial Intelligence applications. Successful candidates will be able to build collaborations with the Elson Floyd College of Medicine at WSU Spokane and Pacific Northwest National Laboratory, as well as other departments at WSU. Additional Information: Nine-Month Academic-Year Salary : $110,000 - $120,000 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications . In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in bioengineering, biomedical engineering, or other engineering disciplines related to bioengineering prior to the start of the appointment. Ability to teach core bioengineering classes, and candidates who have experience in industry or can teach capstone design are particularly encouraged to apply. Demonstrated record of scholarly work and potential to establish a robust nationally recognized, extramurally funded research program. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: May be eligible for work visa sponsorship About Department/College/Area/ Campus - Gene and Linda Voiland School of Chemical Engineering and Bioengineering | Washington State University Area/College: Department Name: The Gene and Linda Voiland School of Chemical Engineering and Bioengineering Location: Pullman, Washington Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. A cover letter A curriculum vita A statement of research plans and potential synergy with VSCEB, other WSU faculty, and WSU Spokane medical community (maximum 2 pages) A statement of teaching experience and interests (maximum of 2 pages) Contact: Inquiries should be directed to Dr. David Lin. the Search Committee chair, by email at davidlin@wsu.edu. Please also cc: Dr. Su Ha, suha@wsu.edu, Director of VSCEB. External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Reference contact information will be requested later in the recruitment process through Workday. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Assistant-Associate-Professor--Career-Track--Faculty-Positions-in-Computer-Science-and-Artificial-Intelligence_R-15533": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor (Career-Track) Faculty Positions in Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: November 1, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 161-NN_FACULTY - Scholarly Assistant Professor - Career, 162-NN_FACULTY - Scholarly Associate Professor - Career, 396-NN_FACULTY - New Teaching Track - Pre-Academic Year Business Title: Assistant/Associate Professor (Career-Track) Faculty Positions in Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence Employee Type: Faculty (+) (Fixed Term) Position Term: 9 Month Position Details: The Opportunity: The School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science (EECS) at Washington State University (WSU) invites applications for multiple full-time, nine-month, Career-Track faculty positions at the Scholarly Assistant Professor or Scholarly Associate Professor rank. The position will be considered for an initial 2-year fixed term appointment with renewal based upon the needs of the program and available funding. The positions will be based at WSU’s main campus in Pullman, Washington. The anticipated start date is January 1, 2027. These positions are primarily teaching-focused intended for candidates who are strongly committed to excellence in undergraduate education, student mentorship, curriculum development, and continuous improvement of academic programs. The school anticipates hiring faculty to support the following areas: Artificial Intelligence: Teaching and curriculum development in areas such as artificial intelligence, machine learning, generative AI, agentic AI, responsible AI/ethics, programming and foundations for AI, AI systems, robotics, and human computer interaction. General Computer Science: Teaching core and advanced undergraduate courses in areas such as programming, data structures, algorithms, databases, software engineering, computer systems, web and mobile development, and related areas. Depending on programmatic needs, successful candidates may teach courses across the Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Software Engineering, Computer Engineering, and Cybersecurity. Duties and Responsibilities The duties of the successful candidates will include: Primarily teaching undergraduate/graduate courses in computer science, artificial intelligence, software engineering, computer engineering, cybersecurity, data analytics, and related areas. Developing new courses and updating existing courses to meet evolving disciplinary, programmatic, and workforce needs. Contributing to the design and implementation of coursework for WSU’s developing Artificial Intelligence program. Participating in continuous teaching assessment, accreditation, and program-improvement activities. Applying evidence-based and student-centered teaching practices. Mentoring, advising, and effectively communicating with students. Contributing to departmental, college, university, and professional service appropriate to a Career-Track faculty appointment. Collaborating respectfully and productively with faculty, staff, students, industry partners, and other stakeholders. Additional Information: Nine-Month Academic-Year Salary Scholarly Assistant Professor: $95,000 - 105,000 Scholarly Associate Professor: $105,000 - 125,000 In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications for Scholarly Assistant Professor: Master’s in Computer Science or a related discipline by December 15, 2026. Demonstrate strong teaching, communication, and leadership skills, with experience or demonstrated potential in developing and delivering curricula in artificial intelligence, computer science, or related areas. Relevant academic or industry experience is preferred.   Preferred Qualifications for Scholarly Assistant Professor: PhD in Computer Science or a related discipline by December 15, 2026. Required Qualifications for Scholarly Associate Professor: Ph.D. in Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Computer Engineering, Software Engineering, Data Science, or a closely related discipline. A sustained record of effective undergraduate teaching and student mentorship. Demonstrated experience in course and curriculum development. Demonstrated academic or professional leadership appropriate for appointment at the Associate Professor rank. Ability to teach courses in artificial intelligence and/or general computer science. Strong communication, collaboration, and leadership skills. Demonstrated commitment to supporting an accessible and inclusive educational environment consistent with WSU’s land-grant mission. Preferred Qualifications for Scholarly Associate Professor: Demonstrated leadership in curriculum development, program assessment, educational innovation, mentoring, and academic, industry, or professional service. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: May be eligible for work visa sponsorship About Department/College/Area/ Campus - https://school.eecs.wsu.edu/ Area/College: Voiland College of Engineering and Architecture Department Name: School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Location: Pullman, WA For questions about these positions, please contact Dr. Parteek Kumar parteek.kumar@wsu.edu Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. A cover letter describing their qualifications and identifying their primary area of interest: Artificial Intelligence, General Computer Science, or Both. A curriculum vitae. A teaching statement describing teaching philosophy, experience, and evidence of teaching effectiveness. Contact information for three professional references. Applicants seeking consideration at the Scholarly Associate Professor rank should clearly describe their record of sustained teaching effectiveness, curriculum leadership, educational innovation, student mentorship, and academic or professional service. External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. About WSU, Pullman, Voiland College of Engineering and Architecture, and The School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Washington State University is one of the nation’s top 50 public research universities in computer science according to U.S. News and World Report . WSU is among 108 public and private universities in America recognized with the “very high research activity” categorization and among 240 universities and colleges nationwide to earn the “community engagement” classification from the Carnegie Foundation. The new faculty will join faculty members in the School of EECS which has experienced rapid growth over the past few years. EECS expects continued increase in enrollments and anticipates multiple faculties hiring over the next few years. The current faculty composition in the School of EECS includes a member of the National Academy of Engineering and twelve fellows of national professional organizations as well as nineteen CAREER and Young Investigator award recipients. Our graduate students have recently won multiple prestigious awards including Microsoft Research fellowship, IBM Research fellowship, and NSF Computing Innovation fellowship. New faculty members will join a collaborative academic community committed to excellence in teaching, curriculum innovation, student success, and engagement with industry and other external partners. The successful candidates will have opportunities to contribute to established computing programs as well as emerging curricular initiatives in artificial intelligence. WSU acknowledges that its locations statewide are on the homelands of Native American peoples, who have lived in this region and have been caretakers of the land from time immemorial. The Morrill Act of 1862 established our land-grant institution by providing public and federal lands that are traced back to the disposition of Indigenous lands, often taken by coercive and violent acts, and the disregard of treaties. For that, we extend our deepest apologies. We owe our deepest gratitude to the Native peoples of this region and maintain our commitment towards reconciliation. And as a land-grant institution, WSU is deeply committed to the land-grant mission, its Memorandums of Understanding with Native tribes in the region, and a tradition of service to society. To read the full WSU land acknowledgement, please see https://wsu.edu/about/wsu-land-acknowledgement/ . Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Career-Track-Assistant-or-Associate-Professor-Construction-Management_R-15499": {
      "title": "Career Track Assistant Professor Construction Management",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted 21 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: February 1, 2027 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 161-NN_FACULTY - Scholarly Assistant Professor - Career, 396-NN_FACULTY - New Teaching Track - Pre-Academic Year Business Title: Career Track Assistant Professor Construction Management Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 9 Month Position Details: The Opportunity: The School of Design and Construction (SDC) at Washington State University (WSU) invites applications for a full-time faculty member as a continuous-career track (non-tenure track) Assistant Professor. We seek candidates who are student-centered, collaborative, possess relevant industry experience, and are technologically and professionally competent. The Construction Management (CM) program exists within the SDC, which is comprised of academic degree programs in Architecture, Construction Management, Interior Design, and Landscape Architecture. The SDC is housed within the Voiland College of Engineering & Architecture (VCEA), providing access and potential collaborations with faculty and students in civil engineering, mechanical engineering, computer science, and more. For more information about the SDC and CM program, visit our homepage: https://sdc.wsu.edu/ . The curriculum is a four-year professional program leading to a Bachelor of Science in Construction Management. Upon completion of prerequisite courses, pre-professional students must apply after their first year to be admitted into the program and complete the final three years. The CM program has a robust in-house student internship and full-time recruiting program, active industry partners, and an engaged advisory board. Additionally, many non-CM students enroll in our CM Minor program from many disciplines including Business, Architecture, Landscape Architecture, Interior Design, and Civil Engineering. Duties and Responsibilities: Teach lecture, lab, and seminar courses central to the core CM curriculum in the CM (B.S.) program and allied disciplines within the SDC. Courses include, but are not limited to, surveying, materials and methods, project management/delivery, construction cost accounting, construction law, estimating, and scheduling. Additionally, faculty are expected to participate in program and SDC committees, mentor students, and serve as a faculty advisor for student groups and/or coach competition teams. The ability to conduct research and scholarly activities in a defined area of interest is beneficial but not required. This is a 100% FTE position with a 60% Teaching, 20% Teaching Scholarship/Research, and 20% Service workload distribution. The successful candidate will be expected to teach courses central to the construction management core curriculum. Those with multidisciplinary teaching interests are encouraged to apply. Additional Information: Academic Year (9-month) Salary:  $95,000 - $105,000 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in construction management, engineering, architecture, or related discipline from an accredited institution. Relevant construction industry experience in the U.S. and a proven record of collaborative work within the construction industry. Demonstrated ability to teach core curriculum courses (e.g. surveying, materials and methods, project management/project delivery, construction cost accounting, construction law, estimating, and scheduling) in the undergraduate B.S. Construction Management program and allied disciplines in the school. Willingness and ability to engage with students and industry partners, including the mentorship of students from a wide range of disciplines and academic backgrounds. Preferred Qualifications: The ideal candidate will have: Five (5) or more years of domestic construction and/or engineering experience, a bachelor’s degree in construction management, engineering, architecture or a related field, as well as a Master of Business Administration or equivalent; OR Five (5) or more years of domestic construction and/or engineering experience, and a master’s degree in construction management, engineering, architecture or a related field; OR Ten (10) or more years of domestic construction and/or engineering experience, and a bachelor’s degree in construction management, engineering, architecture or a related field. Additional Preferred Qualifications: Professional registration/certification such as PE, AIA, LEED, WELL, CPC, PMP, or DBIA. Demonstrated higher education teaching experience in a construction management program. Five (5) or more years of construction industry experience in one or more of the following construction market sectors: commercial, large-scale residential construction, heavy civil, infrastructure, or specialty contracting. Demonstrated interpersonal skills, including collaboration and teamwork. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: Not eligible for work visa sponsorship About Washington State University: Washington State University is a land grant, multiple-campus, Research 1 institution. This position will serve on the Pullman campus, which has an enrollment of around 20,000 students and is located in Southeastern Washington on the homelands of the Nimíipuu (Nez Perce) Tribe and Palus people. Located about 70 miles south of metropolitan Spokane, Pullman is also a quick drive away from the scenic Idaho panhandle and Moscow Mountain. The rolling hills of the Palouse offer a wide range of activities and a true four-season climate. The area provides ample opportunity to enjoy the cultural and academic hub of both Washington State University and the University of Idaho, in the neighboring town of Moscow, Idaho. Find more information about the region online at https://pullmanchamber.com . About School of Design and Construction: The SDC provides a vital academic and research culture where students and faculty engage in complex social, environmental, and technological questions of our time. We seek forward-thinking candidates with the vision, passion, and expertise to advance the field and inspire future leaders. The SDC is comprised of accredited academic degree programs in Construction Management (B.S. ACCE + CM minor), Architecture (B.S. + M.Arch NAAB), Interior Design (B.A. CIDA + M.A. Research), Landscape Architecture (BLA LAAB), a Master of Energy Conscious Construction, and graduate and undergraduate Energy Conscious Construction certificate program. The SDC is committed to being the premier destination for integrated design and construction education in the nation. The collaborative make-up of the SDC enables faculty to work with students from different disciplines in an integrated environment with the goal of promoting and increasing effectiveness within the professions. Interdisciplinary and collaborative opportunities for both teaching and research also extend throughout the VCEA, especially with the Composite Materials & Engineering Center ( www.cmec.wsu.edu ), the Integrated Design + Construction Laboratory ( https://idcl.wsu.edu/ ), and the Departments of Civil and Environmental Engineering, Mechanical and Materials Engineering, and Electrical Engineering & Computer Science. Visit ( www.sdc.wsu.edu ) for more information on the School of Design and Construction. Area/College: Voiland School of Engineering and Architecture Department Name: School of Design and Construction Location: Pullman, WA Position Start Date: August 16, 2027 Application Instructions: All candidates must upload a cover letter and resume/cv to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the candidate meets all required qualifications. Documents may be submitted in one file or separate files. Candidates are encouraged to upload as a PDF if possible. Candidates will be requested to provide contact information for professional references within the online application. External candidates, please upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Internal candidates, please upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. A complete application must include: Cover letter outlining the candidate’s interests, qualifications, pertinent experience, and vision for the position. Document indicating teaching philosophy (two-pages). A detailed curriculum vitae (resume) highlighting educational and professional experience. Reference contact information will be requested during the application process. For questions about the position, please contact Darhl Edwards at darhl.edwards@wsu.edu. First consideration will be provided to applications submitted prior to January 5, 2027. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Tenure-Track-Tenured-Faculty-Position-in-Electrical-Engineering_R-15485": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track/Tenured Faculty Position in Electrical Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted 22 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: November 1, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 270-NN_FACULTY - Assistant Professor, 280-NN_FACULTY - Associate Professor Business Title: Tenure-Track/Tenured Faculty Position in Electrical Engineering Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 9 Month Position Details: The Opportunity: The School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science (EECS) at Washington State University (WSU) on the Pullman, Washington Campus invites applications from emerging scholars to join our community as a permanent, full-time, tenure-track faculty member in Electrical Engineering at the Assistant or Associate Professor level . Although all areas of electrical engineering will be considered, research emphasis will be on power systems, including applications of machine learning and/or cybersecurity to power systems engineering . The electric power engineering program at WSU ranks among the top five in the nation in faculty, student, and research expenditure. WSU’s power program consists of twelve faculty members in EECS and two staff who specialize in teaching and research in electric power engineering. The group includes a member of the National Academy of Engineering and four fellows of IEEE. This core group is complemented by over 20 affiliated faculty members from other departments across the university, together forming the multidisciplinary research center, Energy Systems Innovation Center (ESIC) (https://esic.wsu.edu/). ESIC has a long tradition of close collaboration with the regional power industry and is well-supported by two annual membership programs. The power program’s annual research expenditure is about $3M, with approximately 30 graduate students and multiple postdocs and visiting researchers. The WSU power faculty collaborates closely with the power industry, research institutions worldwide, and various government organizations.   Another strength of the WSU power program is the Advanced Grid Institute (AGI), which is a joint institute between WSU and the PNNL dedicated to modernizing the grid (https://natlab.wsu.edu/grid/). AGI is envisioned to be the leader in modeling and simulation of the power grid to enable advanced controls and operations for the resilient, sustainable power system of the future. WSU is also a member of the Power System Engineering Research Center (PSERC), which is a consortium of 12 leading universities and 27 industry partners. Summary of Duties: The job duties of successful candidates at all levels include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in electrical engineering; conducting funded research; publishing in top-tier venues; directing M.S. and Ph.D. student research programs; participating in the continuous teaching assessment activities of the School of EECS; effectively communicating and interacting with students; and collaborating with others within the School, the University, or other organizations including regional partners, such as the PNNL. Additional Information: Academic year (9-month) Salary: Assistant Professor Salary Range: $100,000 to $115,000 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications Associate Professor Salary Range: $115,001 to $135,000 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications for all candidates at all ranks: Ph.D. degree in Electrical Engineering, or a related field by the time of hire. Demonstrated record of publishing peer-reviewed research work in leading journals and research conferences. Demonstrated strong commitment to building a world-class research program at WSU. Proven or potential for successful teaching and instruction.  Demonstrated record of or potential for commitment to an inclusive educational experience in alignment with WSU’s land grant mission. Additional Required Qualifications for Associate Professor Rank: Must have served at least six (6) years in academia, industry, or a national lab after Ph.D. Must have experience in advising students and leading funded research programs. Preferred Qualifications for all candidates at all ranks: Demonstrated record of accomplishments to become outstanding scholars and educators. Excellent communication skills to be an effective instructor for undergraduate and graduate classes in topics related to electric power engineering. Strong commitment to building a robust research program through extramural funding. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: May be eligible for work visa sponsorship Area/College: Voiland College of Engineering and Architecture Department Name: School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Location: Pullman, WA Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. External candidates, please upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Internal candidates, please upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. A cover letter addressing qualifications for this position Curriculum vitae A research statement that describes research areas of interest, research accomplishments including scholarly work, and future plans A teaching statement that describes your teaching experience and philosophy, which may incorporate experience from formal or informal teaching and mentoring opportunities Inquiries should be directed to Prof. Mani V. Venkatasubramanian, mani@wsu.edu . External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Reference contact information will be requested at time of applying. About WSU, Pullman, VCEA, and EECS : Washington State University is a land-grant, multiple-campus, Research 1 institution. This position will serve on the Pullman campus, which has an enrollment of around 20,000 students and is located in Southeastern Washington on the homelands of the Nimíipuu (Nez Perce) Tribe and Palus people. Located about 70 miles south of metropolitan Spokane, Pullman is also a quick drive away from the scenic Idaho panhandle and Moscow Mountain. The rolling hills of the Palouse offer a wide range of activities and a true four-season climate. The area provides ample opportunity to enjoy the cultural and academic hub of both Washington State University and the University of Idaho, in the neighboring town of Moscow, Idaho. Find more information about the region online at https://pullmanchamber.com . The new faculty member will join 32 existing tenure-track faculty members and 18 career-track faculty members in the School of EECS, which has experienced rapid enrollment growth over the past five years. Around the fall of 2025, over 1,000 undergraduate students were enrolled in its B.S. programs, and 148 graduate students were enrolled in its M.S. and Ph.D. programs in computer science, cybersecurity, electrical engineering, and computer engineering. The School of EECS expects continued enrollment growth and anticipates multiple faculty hires over the next two years. The current faculty composition in the School of EECS includes a member of the National Academy of Engineering, over ten fellows of international professional societies, and eighteen CAREER and Young Investigator award recipients. Our faculty excel in both teaching and research, and we sustain multiple internationally recognized research programs in artificial intelligence/machine learning, electronic design automation and computer architecture, high-performance computing, data science, cybersecurity, software engineering, and power systems and engineering. Our graduate students have recently won multiple prestigious awards, including Microsoft Research fellowship, IBM Research fellowship, and NSF Computing Innovation fellowship. Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU acknowledges that its locations statewide are on the homelands of Native American peoples, who have lived in this region and have been caretakers of the land from time immemorial. The Morrill Act of 1862 established our land-grant institution by providing public and federal lands that are traced back to the disposition of Indigenous lands, often taken by coercive and violent acts, and the disregard of treaties. For that, we extend our deepest apologies. We owe our deepest gratitude to the Native peoples of this region and maintain our commitment towards reconciliation. And as a land-grant institution, WSU is deeply committed to the land-grant mission, its Memorandums of Understanding with Native tribes in the region, and a tradition of service to society. Read the full WSU land acknowledgement online https://wsu.edu/about/wsu-land-acknowledgement/ . Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Assistant-Professor---Structural-Engineering_R-15304": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor | Structural Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted 28 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: October 30, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 270-NN_FACULTY - Assistant Professor Business Title: Assistant Professor | Structural Engineering Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 9 Month - Summer Position Details: The Opportunity: The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering (CEE) at Washington State University (WSU) in Pullman, WA invites applications for a permanent full-time, nine-month, tenure-track faculty position at the level of Assistant Professor.  This position is focused on structural engineering and mechanics. Candidates with expertise in timber engineering, mass timber design, and/or hybrid structural systems are preferred and encouraged to apply. The position is intended to build upon existing strengths in structural engineering in the department and to build upon strengths existing in the Composite Materials & Engineering Center (CMEC), an interdisciplinary research center at WSU supported by CEE structures faculty.  It is anticipated that the successful candidate will begin the appointment on August 16th 2027. An earlier Spring 2027 start date may be negotiable. In this role, you will be expected to teach undergraduate and graduate classes in civil engineering, establish an independent research program through extramurally funded research grants, publish in top tier peer reviewed journals and conferences, recruit and advise MS and PhD graduate students, participate in undergraduate curriculum development and assessment, and collaborate with colleagues in the department, college, university system, and industrial and state partners to support the land grant mission of WSU. You will also be expected to develop and be committed to developing a world class research program. The anticipated workload will be 50% research, 30% teaching, and 20% service. Additional Information: Screening Date: Applicant screening will begin October 1, 2026 9-month Academic Salary: $105,000 - $110,000 USD | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position or as mandated by a U.S. Department of Labor prevailing wage determination. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benfits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: Doctoral degree in Civil Engineering by the time of hire. Demonstrated publication record in structural engineering in top-tier peer reviewed journals and conferences. Experience or displays potential for success in teaching and instruction. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated ability to effectively communicate and work collegially and collaboratively with faculty, staff, and students. Research interest in hybrid or modular structures. Possession of a Professional Engineering (PE) license or demonstrated progress toward licensure . Position Sponsorship Eligibility: May be eligible for work visa sponsorship Area/College: Voiland College of Engineering and Architecture Department Name: Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering Location: Pullman Campus - Pullman, WA Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1) a cover letter addressing qualifications for this position 2) a curriculum vitae 3) 1-page research statement that describes research areas of interest, research accomplishments, and future research plan at WSU 4) 1-page teaching statement that describes any teaching experience you may have and your teaching philosophy, including mentoring of students External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Reference contact information will be requested later in the recruitment process through Workday. Inquires should be directed to Professor Christopher Motter, c.motter@wsu.edu   About WSU Civil and Environmental Engineering - Washington State University (WSU) is a land-grant, multi-campus, Research-1 institution. This position serves on the Pullman campus, which has an enrollment of ~20,000 students. Pullman is a college town located in the Palouse region of south-eastern Washington. The climate is dry and the distinctive rolling hills of the Palouse are home to one of the most productive dryland wheat growing regions of the world. The location has unique recreational access to forests, rivers, and mountains in Idaho, Oregon, and WA. People who work at WSU Pullman typically live either in Pullman or the neighboring town of Moscow, Idaho, nine miles away. The larger metropolitan area of Spokane is 70 miles to the north. The CEE Department is one of five schools/departments in the Voiland College of Engineering and Architecture. The department has over 300 undergraduate students, 50 graduate students, and 22 tenured/tenure-track faculty members and 3 career track faculty. Degrees offered are Bachelor of Science in Civil Engineering (BSCE), Master of Science in Civil Engineering (MSCE), Master of Science in Environmental Engineering (MSCE), a PhD degree in Civil Engineering, and a PhD in Engineering Science. The CEE Department is in the PACCAR Environmental Technology Building that houses research and teaching labs and classrooms. In addition, the CMEC has material testing labs in PACCAR as well as the ICC-accredited Simpson Strong-Tie Structural and Research Laboratory (SSSRL). This testing facility features a two-story tall reaction wall with a through-hole pattern to support actuators up to 200 kips and a 40 ft × 60 ft reaction floor with a 2 ft × 2 ft anchorage grid for adaptable test setups. The lab is equipped with a dual-hook 7.5-ton overhead crane and servo-hydraulic actuators ranging from 10 to 300 kips with 10-48 in. stroke lengths. An additional 40 ft × 60 ft outdoor reaction floor supports full-scale construction of prototypes or testing of large structures. Additional information about CMEC facilities can be found online about the CMEC Equipment & International Code Council Accredited Testing Laboratory. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Assistant-Professor---Career-Track---Criminal-Justice---Criminology--Pullman--WA-_R-15459": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Career Track | Criminal Justice & Criminology (Pullman, WA)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-21\nPosted: Posted 30 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: October 23, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 158-NN_FACULTY - Teaching Assistant Professor - Career Business Title: Assistant Professor - Career Track | Criminal Justice & Criminology (Pullman, WA) Employee Type: Faculty (+) (Fixed Term) Position Term: 9 Month Position Details: The Opportunity: Department of Criminal Justice and Criminology, College of Arts and Sciences is pleased to announce a search for a full-time 9-month academic year Assistant Professor (Teaching), Career Track faculty position in Pullman, WA. This position is a 3-year, fixed-term appointment with the possibility of renewal. We seek applicants to teach courses in multiple areas of criminal justice, with a preference for those applicants with experience and interest in teaching in the areas of law and courts. The job duties will include primarily teaching introductory and upper-division level courses within the Criminal Justice and Criminology curriculum on the Pullman campus and through the Global campus, which is dedicated to online education. You may also potentially teach graduate seminars. Additionally, you will mentor students, develop and implement high-impact pedagogical practices, and conduct departmental and college service as appropriate. Your teaching load will be four courses per semester (4/4), representing 80% of the position, with an additional 20% devoted to service. It is anticipated that the successful candidate will begin their appointment on August 16, 2027. Additional Information: Screening of applications will begin immediately thereafter and continue until the position is filled. This is a full time (100% FTE), 9-month , fixed-term appointment for 3 years, with the possibility of renewal. 9-Month Academic Year Salary: $60,000.00 - $67,000.00 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the Detailed Summary of Benefits for WSU Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: Ph.D. or J.D. ABD will be considered. PhD/ABD must be in criminal justice, criminology, or a related discipline. Degree must be earned by appointment start date. Preferred Qualifications: Ph.D. in criminal justice, criminology, or a related discipline. Experience teaching legal and/or court related courses. Experience teaching online.  Position Sponsorship Eligibility: Not eligible for work visa sponsorship Inquiries should be directed to: Professor Dale Willits (dale.willits@wsu.edu), Search Committee Chair. Area/College: College of Arts and Sciences Department Name: Criminal Justice and Criminology Location: Pullman, WA Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1) A Letter of Application addressing qualifications for this position; 2)   Curriculum Vitae 3)  Teaching Portfolio, which at a minimum must include a statement of teaching philosophy. Other relevant information could include a list of university courses taught independently, prior syllabi, and teaching evaluation summaries; 4) Names, addresses, and contact information of at least three references who can address your history of and potential for excellence in teaching and service, which will be collected within the system at a later stage of the recruitment process (letters will be requested at a later time for short-list candidates). External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Reference contact information will be requested later in the recruitment process through Workday. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Assistant-Professor_R-15327": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: January 31, 2027 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 270-NN_FACULTY - Assistant Professor Business Title: Assistant Professor Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 9 Month - Summer Position Details: The Opportunity: The School of Mechanical and Materials Engineering (MME) in the Voiland College of Engineering and Architecture (VCEA) at Washington State University invites applications for a full-time, 9-month, tenure-track position at the Assistant Professor rank in Pullman, WA. The successful candidate is expected to, in collaboration with the current faculty, play a leading role in building interdisciplinary teams with substantial external research funding in the respective area and be an effective instructor for undergraduate and graduate classes in mechanical engineering. The department seeks to build upon our core strengths and serve the needs of our state and region in the thermal-fluids research area, including, but not limited to, interfacial transport phenomena, waste thermal energy recovery, multiphase flows and phase-change heat transfer, reacting flows in extreme environments, packaging and data center thermal management. Applicants must show a record of accomplishments that demonstrates their potential to become outstanding scholars and educators, build a robust research program, and embody excellent communication skills. The typical workload for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position within MME is 40% research, 40% teaching, and 20% service. Additional Information: This is a full-time (100% FTE) , 9-month, tenure-track   faculty position. This position is   not eligible for overtime. Screening date: First consideration will be provided to applications submitted before November 1, 2026. Review of applications will remain ongoing until the posting close date of January 31, 2027. 9-month Academic Salary: $100,000 - $115,000 USD | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position, or as mandated by a U.S. Department of Labor prevailing wage determination.   Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: Completed Ph.D. degree in Mechanical Engineering, Materials Science and Engineering, or a related field by date of hire. Demonstrated strong commitment to building a world-class research program at WSU through publication of peer-reviewed research in leading journals and conferences, with potential to secure external research funding. Proven or potential for successful teaching and instruction. Demonstrated record of or potential for commitment to equitable and inclusive educational experiences and service in alignment with WSU’s land grant mission. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated record of accomplishments to become outstanding scholars and educators. Excellent communication skills to be an effective instructor for undergraduate and graduate classes in mechanical engineering or materials science and engineering. Demonstrated record or potential to become a national leader in the field of research. Strong commitment to build a robust research program through extramural funding. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: May be eligible for work visa sponsorship About Department/College/Area/ Campus - learn more about the School of Mechanical and Materials Engineering Area/College: Voiland College of Engineering and Architecture Department Name: School of Mechanical and Materials Engineering Location: Pullman Campus - Pullman, WA Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. A cover letter addressing qualifications for this position as well as a response to the following prompt: \"As a land-grant university, our university has a continuing commitment to ensuring equitable and meaningful inclusion in our classrooms, collaborations, and research excellence. Please explain how your research and teaching interests align with our mission.\" Curriculum vitae Research statement (3 pages maximum) describing the applicant’s research accomplishments and future research plans Teaching statement (2 pages maximum) describing the applicant’s teaching experience, philosophy, and interests Name, addresses, and contact information of at least four references who can address your history of and potential for excellence in research, teaching, and service (letters will be requested at a later time). External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application.   Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Contact: Inquiries should be directed to Professor Jacob Leachman, jacob.leachman@wsu.edu. About WSU, Pullman, VCEA, and MME: Washington State University, a land-grant, multiple-campus Research 1 institution. This position will serve on the Pullman campus, which has an enrollment of around 20,000 students and is located in Southeastern Washington on the homelands of the Nimíipuu (Nez Perce) Tribe and Palus people. Located 80 miles south of metropolitan Spokane, Pullman is also a quick drive away from the scenic Idaho panhandle and Moscow Mountain. The rolling hills of the Palouse offer a wide range of activities and a true four-season climate. The area provides ample opportunity to enjoy the cultural and academic hub of both Washington State University and the University of Idaho, in the neighboring town of Moscow, Idaho. With a population of 34,000, Pullman is a quintessential college town ranked #1 in the “Best Cities to Live in Washington” by the Washington State Chamber of Commerce 2019 report; and recognized by Bloomberg Businessweek as the “Best Place to Raise Kids” in Washington state. For more information about the region, please refer to https://pullmanchamber.com . Washington State University is a recipient of a National Science Foundation ADVANCE Institutional Transformation Award to increase the participation of women in academic science and engineering careers. Washington State University is building a culturally diverse faculty and strongly encourages applications from women and minority groups. Washington State University is also pro-active in exploring opportunities for the employment of spouses, both inside and outside the University. MME currently has over 1000 students and 40 faculty members. The current annual extramural research expenditure in MME is nearly $14 million. With the motto, “Work ready day one,” the School of MME prides itself in its collegial environment, with multiple campuses across the state, close to key industry partners in nuclear, aerospace, and naval engineering. Our students are world-class, with many winning nationally and internationally competitive awards as individuals or teams. About one-quarter of our undergraduate students participate in engineering clubs, and many participate in undergraduate research. Our faculty exhibits excellence in both teaching and research, and we sustain multiple internationally recognized research programs in healthcare, manufacturing, energy, robotics, and advanced materials. MME has unique research centers, including the Nuclear Science Center, the Institute of Materials Research, the Hydrogen Properties for Energy Research Center, and the Institute for Shock Physics. The proximity of the Pullman campus to the Pacific Northwest National Laboratory (PNNL) provides exciting opportunities for our students and faculty, including joint appointments with PNNL for successful candidates. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Assistant-Professor_R-15328": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: January 31, 2027 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 270-NN_FACULTY - Assistant Professor Business Title: Assistant Professor Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 9 Month - Summer Position Details: The Opportunity: The School of Mechanical and Materials Engineering (MME) in the Voiland College of Engineering and Architecture (VCEA) at Washington State University invites applications for a full-time, 9-month, tenure-track position at the Assistant Professor rank in Pullman, WA. MME seeks to strengthen its capabilities in robotics and autonomous systems, with an emphasis on intelligent robotic systems designed to operate in complex, unstructured, and dynamic environments. The search is intentionally broad across robotics and autonomy. Relevant application domains include, but are not limited to, agriculture, advanced manufacturing, environmental monitoring, nuclear systems, underwater and marine robotics, infrastructure inspection, construction, disaster response, and other real-world and mission-critical applications. Candidates whose work can complement and create new collaborations with MME’s existing strengths in advanced manufacturing, materials, energy, nuclear engineering, and related areas are particularly encouraged. It is anticipated the successful candidate will begin their appointment on August 16, 2027. Job Duties: The successful candidate is expected to establish an internationally recognized, externally funded research program that advances the autonomy, intelligence, robustness, and reliability of next-generation robotic systems. The candidate will collaborate with current faculty to build interdisciplinary teams and contribute to excellence in teaching, mentoring, and service. We seek candidates with a strong foundation in robotics and autonomous systems. Areas of expertise may include, but are not limited to, robot perception, sensing, navigation and localization, planning and control, manipulation, robot learning, embodied AI, human–robot interaction, multi-robot systems, mechatronics, robotic system integration, and autonomous decision-making for physical robotic systems operating in real-world environments. Candidates who integrate artificial intelligence and machine learning with physical robotic systems are particularly encouraged to apply. Research that connects robotics with the School’s existing strengths—including advanced manufacturing and materials—is welcomed, but the candidate’s primary scholarly identity should be in robotics and autonomous systems. The successful candidate should be capable of developing and teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in robotics and related areas, including mechatronics, system dynamics, control systems, introduction to robotics, robot dynamics, and other courses aligned with the department’s educational mission and the candidate’s expertise. The typical workload for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position within MME is 40% research, 40% teaching, and 20% service. Additional Information: This is a full-time (100% FTE) , 9-month, tenure-track  faculty position. This position is   not eligible for overtime . Screening date: First consideration will be provided to applications submitted before November 1, 2026. Review of applications will remain ongoing until the posting close date of January 31, 2027. 9-month Academic Salary: $100,000 - $115,000 USD | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position, or as mandated by a U.S. Department of Labor prevailing wage determination. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: Ph.D. degree in Mechanical Engineering, Robotics Engineering, Electrical Engineering, or a related field by the date of hire. Demonstrate a strong commitment to building a world-class research program at WSU through publication of peer-reviewed research in leading journals and conferences, with potential to secure external research funding. Proven or potential for successful teaching and instruction. Demonstrated record of or potential for commitment to equitable and inclusive educational experiences and service in alignment with WSU’s land grant mission. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated record of accomplishments and potential to become an outstanding scholar and educator in robotics and autonomous systems. Excellent communication skills and the ability to teach undergraduate and graduate courses in robotics and related areas, such as mechatronics, dynamics, controls, and robot dynamics. Demonstrated record or potential to become a national and international leader in robotics and autonomous systems, particularly in the development of intelligent robotic systems for real-world, unstructured, and dynamic environments. Strong commitment to building a robust, externally funded research program and fostering interdisciplinary collaborations, including opportunities to connect robotics with MME strengths in advanced manufacturing, materials, energy, nuclear engineering, and related areas. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: May be eligible for work visa sponsorship About Department/College/Area/ Campus - learn more about the School of Mechanical and Materials Engineering Area/College: Voiland College of Engineering and Architecture Department Name: School of Mechanical and Materials Engineering Location: WSU Pullman Campus - Pullman, WA Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. A cover letter addressing qualifications for this position as well as a response to the following prompt: \"As a land-grant university, our university has a continuing commitment to ensuring equitable and meaningful inclusion in our classrooms, collaborations, and research excellence. Please explain how your research and teaching interests align with our mission.\" Curriculum vitae. Research statement (3 pages maximum) describing the applicant’s research accomplishments and future research plans. Teaching statement (2 pages maximum) describing the applicant’s teaching experience, philosophy, and interests. Name, addresses, and contact information of at least four references who can address your history of and potential for excellence in research, teaching, and service (letters will be requested at a later time). External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Contact: Inquiries should be directed to Dr. Ming Luo, ming.luo@wsu.edu. About WSU, Pullman, VCEA, and MME: Washington State University, a land-grant, multiple-campus Research 1 institution. This position will serve on the Pullman campus, which has an enrollment of around 20,000 students and is located in Southeastern Washington on the homelands of the Nimíipuu (Nez Perce) Tribe and Palus people. Located 80 miles south of metropolitan Spokane, Pullman is also a quick drive away from the scenic Idaho panhandle and Moscow Mountain. The rolling hills of the Palouse offer a wide range of activities and a true four-season climate. The area provides ample opportunity to enjoy the cultural and academic hub of both Washington State University and the University of Idaho, in the neighboring town of Moscow, Idaho. With a population of 34,000, Pullman is a quintessential college town—ranked #1 in the “Best Cities to Live in Washington” by the Washington State Chamber of Commerce 2019 report; and recognized by Bloomberg Businessweek as the “Best Place to Raise Kids” in Washington state. For more information about the region, please refer to https://pullmanchamber.com . Washington State University is a recipient of a National Science Foundation ADVANCE Institutional Transformation Award to increase the participation of women in academic science and engineering careers. Washington State University is building a culturally diverse faculty and strongly encourages applications from women and minority groups. Washington State University is also pro-active in exploring opportunities for the employment of spouses, both inside and outside the University. MME currently has over 1000 students and 40 faculty members. The current annual extramural research expenditure in MME is nearly $14 million. With the motto, “Work ready day one,” the School of MME (www.mme.wsu.edu) prides itself in its collegial environment, with multiple campuses across the state, close to key industry partners in nuclear, aerospace, and naval engineering. Our students are world-class, with many winning nationally and internationally competitive awards as individuals or teams. About one-quarter of our undergraduate students participate in engineering clubs, and many participate in undergraduate research. Our faculty exhibits excellence in both teaching and research, and we sustain multiple internationally recognized research programs in healthcare, manufacturing, energy, robotics, and advanced materials. MME has unique research centers, including the Nuclear Science Center, the Institute of Materials Research, the Hydrogen Properties for Energy Research Center, and the Institute for Shock Physics. The proximity of the Pullman campus to the Pacific Northwest National Laboratory (PNNL) provides exciting opportunities for our students and faculty, including joint appointments with PNNL for successful candidates. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Pullman-WA/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate---McLaughlin-lab_R-14755": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - McLaughlin lab",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: November 8, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 306-YY_FACULTY - Postdoctoral Research Associate Business Title: Postdoctoral Research Associate - McLaughlin lab Employee Type: Faculty (+) (Fixed Term) Position Term: 12 Month Position Details: The Opportunity: The Department of Integrative Physiology and Neuroscience (IPN) at Washington State University (WSU) invites you to apply for a Postdoctoral Research Associate in the Cannabis, Brain, & Development (CBD) Lab , located in Dr. Ryan McLaughlin's lab. As the incumbent, you will apply a multidisciplinary approach with rats to examine the long-term effects of prenatal cannabis exposure on corticostriatal circuit function and cognitive flexibility. Responsibilities include conducting behavioral neuroscience experiments—including vapor self-administration and operant conditioning tasks (such as attentional set-shifting and reversal learning)—performing stereotaxic surgeries, viral (AAV) injections, and optic fiber implants for fiber photometry recordings, and managing a rodent breeding colony. Preference will be given to candidates with a background in developmental neurobiology, cannabinoid pharmacology, and in vivo calcium imaging (fiber photometry). You should have experience or a strong desire to learn ex vivo electrophysiology. The postdoctoral researcher is also expected to demonstrate leadership within the lab, including mentoring graduate and undergraduate students. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . For information or questions about Benefits, please contact HRS Benefits at 509-335-4521. Additional Information: Monthly Salary: $5,186 - $5,862 | Commensurate with experience and education In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. This is a full-time (100% FTE), temporary position. This position is overtime eligible. Appointment Duration:  This is a temporary position expected to start on June 1, 2026 and end on December 31, 2027 . Renewal is dependent on the need for extension and/or if additional funding is secured to extend employment. Postdoctoral Requirements: For detailed information on eligibility for postdoctoral appointments, please visit: WSU/UAW Postdoctoral Collective Bargaining Agreement. Required Qualifications: An earned PhD in Neuroscience or other relevant discipline. Possess strong written and oral communication skills, demonstrate a record of accomplishments, and have less than five years of research experience since earning their doctoral degree. Leadership skills and an interest in mentoring students are required. Preferred Qualifications: Preference will be given to candidates with a background in developmental neurobiology, cannabinoid pharmacology, and in vivo calcium imaging (fiber photometry). Candidates should have experience or a strong desire to learn ex vivo electrophysiology. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: Not eligible for work visa sponsorship About Department/College/Area/ Campus: https://vetmed.wsu.edu/our-team/wsu-profile/ryan.mclaughlin/ Area/College: College of Veterinary Medicine Department Name: Integrative Physiology and Neuroscience Location: Pullman, Washington Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. All candidates , documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Reference will be requested during the application process. Contact : For questions regarding this position or the application process, please contact Laura Kinslow , kinslow@wsu.edu / 509-335-2415 . Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Union Representation: This position is represented exclusively by Postdocs United/United Automobile, Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers of America (UAW) under the Collective Bargaining Agreement (CBA). Information regarding the Union and the CBA is available on the  WSU Labor Relations website. Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
